# XSH regcomp(), regexec(), regerror(), regfree()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The tests run in the POSIX locale (they never call setlocale()), so range
# expressions, equivalence classes and the character classes have the
# POSIX-locale meaning that XBD 9.3.5 gives. Only what XBD chapter 9 states
# is required: constructs it calls undefined or unspecified are not used,
# except in the assertions marked disputed, which rest on the error
# constants of regcomp() for syntax errors that XBD does not call "invalid".
# REG_STARTEND, asctime_r-style extras and other extensions are not on the
# page and are not tested. Failure of regcomp() is accepted with any
# nonzero code: the page lets REG_BADPAT stand for every error.

assertion  XSH.regcomp.001
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Unless REG_NOSUB is given, regcomp() sets re_nsub to the number of
           parenthesized subexpressions: groups written with backslash-
           parentheses in a BRE, plain parentheses in an ERE, nested ones
           included; escaped or bracketed parentheses and, in a BRE, plain
           ones do not count.
test       t/001-nsub.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       A BRE of ordinary characters, period, bracket expressions,
           asterisk and concatenation matches as described: bb* finds
           characters two to four of abbbc; a* on baaa matches the empty
           string at offset 0; a period needs a character; [ab]* and [ab][ab]
           agree on ab.
test       t/002-bre-basic.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      required
since      1992
says       The match begins at the earliest possible position and, among
           those, is the longest: (wee|week)(knights|night) takes all ten
           characters of weeknights, with the first group wee and the second
           knights; abba|cde on abbcde finds cde; a|ab on abc finds ab; the
           sub-matches follow from the overall longest choice.
test       t/003-leftmost-longest.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       BRE interval expressions \{m\}, \{m,\} and \{m,n\} repeat the
           preceding item exactly m, at least m, and m to n times: on
           abababccccccd, c\{3\} is characters seven to nine, \(ab\)\{4,\}
           does not match and c\{1,3\}d is characters ten to thirteen; zero
           counts and bracket or group operands work too.
test       t/004-bre-interval.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.6
class      required
since      1992
says       ERE +, *, ? and intervals repeat the preceding item as stated:
           b+(bc) on acabbbcde is characters four to seven; b*c on cabbbcde
           is the first character; b*cd on cabbbcdebbbbbbcdbc is characters
           three to seven; b?c on acabbbcde is the second character; c{3} and
           (ab){2,} on abababccccccd are characters seven to nine and one to
           six.
test       t/005-ere-dup.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.7
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.8
class      required
since      1992
says       ERE alternation matches either side, a((bc)|d) matches abc and ad,
           alternation has the lowest precedence (ab|cd is ab or cd, not a
           b-or-c d), and a repeated group of alternatives matches a run of
           them.
test       t/006-ere-alternation.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.8
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.3
class      required
since      1992
says       In a BRE a leading circumflex anchors at the start of the string
           and a trailing dollar sign at the end (^ab matches abcdef but not
           cdefab; ab$ the reverse; ^abcdef$ only the whole string); a
           circumflex or dollar sign elsewhere in the expression, or a
           leading dollar sign, is an ordinary character.
test       t/007-bre-anchors.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.9
class      required
since      1992
says       In an ERE circumflex and dollar sign are anchors wherever they
           appear outside a bracket expression, also inside groups and next
           to alternation: ^ab and (^ab) match abcdef but not cdefab, ef$ and
           (ef$) the converse, and a^b and e$f are valid but can never match.
test       t/008-ere-anchors.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.3
class      required
since      1992
says       In a BRE an asterisk that is the first character of the
           expression, follows a leading circumflex, or is the first
           character of a group matches itself.
test       t/009-bre-star-literal.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.2
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.5.1
class      required
since      1992
says       A backslash before a BRE special character (period, asterisk,
           bracket, backslash, circumflex, dollar sign) or before a closing
           bracket, and before any ERE special character, closing bracket or
           closing brace, makes it match itself.
test       t/010-escapes.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.4
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.4
class      required
since      1992
says       A period outside a bracket expression matches every one of the
           characters 1 to 255 of the POSIX locale (a newline included when
           REG_NEWLINE is not given), in a BRE and an ERE, and does not match
           the empty string.
test       t/011-period.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In a BRE, a matching list matches one of its characters and a non-
           matching list any other; a closing bracket first in the list is a
           member; period, asterisk, backslash and left bracket are ordinary
           inside a list; a circumflex not first is a member.
test       t/012-bracket-bre.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.013
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In an ERE bracket expression the operators ( * + ? { | $ . and
           backslash lose their meaning, a circumflex inside is not an anchor
           and a closing bracket first is a member.
test       t/013-bracket-ere.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.014
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale a hyphen first or last in a list is itself, a
           range covers the characters between its endpoints in code order
           ([a-c], [%--], [--@], [0-9]), and a collating symbol can be a
           range start ([][.-.]-0] holds the closing bracket and the
           characters from hyphen to 0); this holds in a BRE and an ERE, and
           a non-matching list is the complement.
test       t/014-ranges.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.015
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       In the POSIX locale a collating symbol [.a.] stands for the
           character, including punctuation ones such as [.-.] and [.].]; an
           equivalence class [=a=] matches a and a list mixing them with
           ordinary characters matches any of the members.
test       t/015-collating.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.016
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
class      required
since      1992
says       The twelve classes alnum, alpha, blank, cntrl, digit, graph,
           lower, print, punct, space, upper and xdigit are recognised; in
           the POSIX locale each matches exactly the characters for which the
           corresponding ctype function is true, a non-matching list matches
           the rest, and classes mix with ordinary list members.
test       t/016-classes.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.017
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
class      required
since      1992
says       A BRE back-reference \n matches the text the nth group matched
           last: ^\(.*\)\1$ matches adjacent repeats, \(a\)*\1 does not match
           a, \(a\(b\)*\)*\2 does not match abab, ^\(ab*\)*\1$ matches
           ababbabb but not ababbab; a group that matched nothing does not
           satisfy a back-reference, and an empty match can be repeated.
test       t/017-backrefs.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.018
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       pmatch[0] holds the offsets of the whole match and pmatch[i] those
           of the subexpression whose opening parenthesis is the ith,
           counting nested groups by their opening parenthesis; offsets are
           bytes from the start of the string, the end being one past the
           last byte.
test       t/018-offsets.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.019
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       Elements of pmatch from the last subexpression up to nmatch-1 that
           correspond to no subexpression of the pattern have both rm_so and
           rm_eo equal to -1.
test       t/019-unused-minus1.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.020
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       When nmatch is smaller than the number of subexpressions plus one,
           regexec() still decides the match against the whole expression (a
           string failing in a later group gives REG_NOMATCH), reports only
           the first nmatch elements and writes no further element.
test       t/020-nmatch-small.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.021
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With nmatch 0, or with REG_NOSUB given to regcomp(), regexec()
           does not use pmatch: a null pointer is accepted and a supplied
           array is left unchanged, while the match result is still reported.
test       t/021-pmatch-ignored.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.022
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A subexpression outside any other that took part in the match
           several times is reported with the offsets of its last occurrence:
           (a|b)* on abab gives 3 and 4 for group one; likewise for + ,
           intervals, and BRE asterisk repetition.
test       t/022-last-match.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.023
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A subexpression that did not take part in an otherwise successful
           match, because a star, question mark or interval allowed zero
           repetitions or because another alternative was chosen, has -1 in
           both members of its pmatch element.
test       t/023-non-participating.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.024
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A group inside a group that is reported as -1 is also -1; a group
           inside a participating group is judged within the last substring
           reported for the outer group: (a(b)*)* on aba gives group one at
           offsets 2 to 3 and group two as -1, though an earlier repetition
           of the outer group matched b.
test       t/024-nested.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.025
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A subexpression that matched the empty string has both offsets
           equal to the offset of the character, or of the terminating null,
           that follows it.
test       t/025-zero-length.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.026
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      required
since      1992
says       Within the overall longest match each subpattern, left to right,
           takes the longest string possible and an empty match counts as
           longer than none: \(.*\).* on abcdef gives group one the whole
           string, \(a*\)* on bc gives it the empty string at 0, and
           (a|ab)(c|bcd)(d*) on abcd gives ab, c, d.
test       t/026-longest-sub.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.027
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.1
class      required
since      1992
says       With REG_ICASE a string that matches case-sensitively still
           matches with any of its characters replaced by their other-case
           counterpart: literals, ranges, the lower and upper classes and
           back-references all follow; without the flag case matters.
test       t/027-icase.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.028
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With REG_NOSUB regexec() still reports match (0) or REG_NOMATCH
           correctly, for BREs and EREs with groups, intervals and anchors,
           whatever nmatch is.
test       t/028-nosub.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.029
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.2
class      required
since      1992
says       Without REG_NEWLINE a newline in pattern or string is ordinary: a
           period and a non-matching list match it and the anchors act at the
           ends of the string only. With it a period and a non-matching list
           do not match a newline in the string, a circumflex anchor also
           matches just after a newline and a dollar anchor just before one,
           whatever REG_NOTBOL and REG_NOTEOL say, and a newline in the
           pattern still matches a newline.
test       t/029-newline.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.030
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With REG_NOTBOL a circumflex anchor does not match at the start of
           the string (in a BRE and an ERE) while expressions without anchors
           and a circumflex that is an ordinary character are unaffected; the
           idiom of searching on with REG_NOTBOL after the first match finds
           each match once.
test       t/030-notbol.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.031
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With REG_NOTEOL a dollar anchor does not match at the end of the
           string; expressions without anchors and a dollar that is an
           ordinary character are unaffected, and both flags can be given
           together.
test       t/031-noteol.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.032
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.2
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.3
class      required
since      1992
says       Without REG_EXTENDED the characters + ? | ( ) { } are ordinary
           while a * and \( \) still work; with it they are operators.
test       t/032-bre-vs-ere.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.033
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.6
class      required
since      2024
says       In an ERE a duplication symbol followed by a question mark (the
           repetition modifier) selects the leftmost shortest match for that
           repetition, including the empty one: .*c on abc abc ends at the
           last c and .*?c at the first; (.*?).* gives group one the empty
           string.
test       t/033-lazy-modifier.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.034
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.6
anchor     basedefs/regex.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With REG_EXTENDED and REG_MINIMAL the leftmost shortest match is
           the default for every duplication symbol and the repetition
           modifier ? selects the longest instead; REG_MINIMAL is a flag bit
           distinct from the others.
test       t/034-minimal.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.035
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       A compiled expression can be used for any number of regexec()
           calls with different strings and flags, interleaved with another
           one; regexec() returns 0 for a match and exactly REG_NOMATCH
           (nonzero) for none.
test       t/035-reuse.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.036
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.2
class      required
since      1992
says       Regular expressions of up to 256 bytes are accepted and work, for
           a BRE and an ERE, including ones with many groups, which re_nsub
           counts.
test       t/036-length-256.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.037
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.4.6
class      required
since      1992
says       Interval counts up to 255 are accepted by regcomp() and mean what
           they say in a BRE and an ERE.
test       t/037-dup-max.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.038
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.6
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       A BRE back-reference \n with fewer than n groups before it is
           invalid, and regcomp() fails with a nonzero code.
test       t/038-bad-backref.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.039
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.3.5
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       A collating symbol that names no collating element of the POSIX
           locale makes the expression invalid, and regcomp() fails with a
           nonzero code.
test       t/039-bad-collating.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.040
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      disputed
since      1992
says       A BRE with an unclosed backslash-parenthesis and an ERE with an
           unclosed parenthesis are rejected by regcomp() with a nonzero
           code.
note       disputed (Q-162): the error constants list a parenthesis imbalance,
           but XBD calls such a grammar violation undefined, not invalid, so
           an implementation could read the stray parenthesis as a literal; a
           FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/040-eparen.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.041
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      disputed
since      1992
says       Interval expressions that are unclosed, not numeric or with the
           lower bound above the upper are rejected by regcomp() with a
           nonzero code.
note       disputed (Q-162): the interval error constants describe an unclosed
           or non-numeric brace pair and a first bound larger than the
           second, which XBD does not call invalid; a FAIL is logged as
           INSPECT.
test       t/041-ebrace.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.042
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      disputed
since      1992
says       An unclosed bracket expression and a character class name that no
           locale defines are rejected by regcomp() with a nonzero code.
note       disputed (Q-162): the bracket and class-type error constants cover
           an unclosed bracket expression and an unknown class name, which
           XBD treats as grammar violations (undefined); a FAIL is logged as
           INSPECT.
test       t/042-ebrack.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.043
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      disputed
since      1992
says       A reversed range expression and an ERE with a repetition operator
           at the start or after an alternation bar are rejected by regcomp()
           with a nonzero code.
note       disputed (Q-162): REG_ERANGE and REG_BADRPT describe a bad range
           endpoint and a repetition operator with nothing before it, but XBD
           calls a leading ERE duplication symbol undefined and does not call
           a reversed range invalid; a FAIL is logged as INSPECT.
test       t/043-erange-badrpt.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.044
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap09.html#9.1
class      disputed
since      1992
says       A pattern ending in a single backslash is rejected by regcomp()
           with a nonzero code.
note       disputed (Q-162): REG_EESCAPE describes a trailing backslash, but
           XBD defines an escape sequence as a backslash and a following
           character and does not call the lone backslash invalid; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/044-eescape.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.045
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       For an error code from regcomp() or regexec() (with the preg of
           that call) regerror() returns the number of bytes needed for the
           whole message including the null, writes the message there when it
           fits, and the message consists of printable characters.
test       t/045-regerror-size.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.046
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       When the buffer is too small regerror() stores the leading part of
           the message cut to the buffer size minus one and a null, writes
           nothing beyond the buffer, and still returns the size of the whole
           message; with a buffer of exactly the needed size the message is
           whole.
test       t/046-regerror-truncate.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.047
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       With errbuf_size 0 regerror() ignores errbuf, writes nothing, and
           returns the size needed (at least 1), the same value as a full-
           size call.
test       t/047-regerror-size0.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.048
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       regerror() with a null preg still produces a printable, null-
           terminated message for a code returned by regcomp() or regexec(),
           with a consistent size.
test       t/048-regerror-nullpreg.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.049
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      disputed
since      1992
says       The message regerror() produces for an error code that regcomp()
           or regexec() returned is not empty, with and without a preg.
note       disputed (Q-163): the page calls the messages unspecified printable
           strings and does not say they are non-empty; an empty message
           would defeat the purpose but is not excluded in words; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT.
test       t/049-regerror-nonempty.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.050
anchor     functions/regcomp.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       regfree() does not change errno for a regex_t returned by
           regcomp() (whatever flags it was compiled with), and the same
           regex_t can be compiled into again afterwards.
note       The errno sentence was added in Issue 8 (Austin Group Defect 385).
test       t/050-regfree-errno.c

assertion  XSH.regcomp.051
anchor     basedefs/regex.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       The error constants are nonzero and pairwise distinct, the compile
           flags are nonzero and do not share bits, as the evaluation flags
           REG_NOTBOL and REG_NOTEOL; regoff_t is signed and wide enough for
           ptrdiff_t and ssize_t values; re_nsub is a size_t and rm_so and
           rm_eo are regoff_t.
test       t/051-constants.c
